# Idiomatic Rust

## Ownership and borrowing

- Parameters: `&str`, `&Path`, `&[T]`, `&dyn Trait`. Take `String` / `PathBuf` / `Vec<T>` only when the function stores the value.
- Return owned values; the caller decides whether to borrow.
- `clone()` only when a borrow cannot work. A `.clone()` inside a loop needs a comment.
- `impl Into<String>` / `impl AsRef<Path>` on constructors that store; keep internal helpers monomorphic.

```rust
// ❌ BAD: forces every caller to allocate
fn label(name: String) -> String

// ✅ GOOD
fn label(name: &str) -> String
```

## State

- Immutable by default: compute, then bind. `let mut` only in the scope that mutates.
- Interior mutability only across a real boundary: `Cell` / `RefCell` single-thread, `Mutex` / `RwLock` / atomics across threads (`worker::SharedProgress`). Never `RefCell` to dodge the borrow checker.
- No global mutable state (`static mut`, `lazy_static` with a `Mutex`); pass state explicitly so it can be faked in tests.

## Types

- Newtypes for values with invariants (`Password`, `StoreList`) instead of bare `String` / `Vec`.
- `enum` + exhaustive `match` instead of boolean flags: `Overwrite::{Always, Never, Rename}`, not `overwrite: bool, rename: bool`.
- Option structs implement `Default`; callers use struct-update syntax: `CompressOpts { level: 1, ..Default::default() }`. Use a builder only when construction has steps that can fail.
- Collections: `Vec` by default, `HashMap` / `HashSet` for lookup, `BTreeMap` / `BTreeSet` when order matters (listings, snapshots).
- Pattern vocabulary when designing: Strategy = trait object chosen at runtime (`ArchiveWriter` per format); Factory = `create(path, fmt, opts) -> Box<dyn ArchiveWriter>`; Observer = `ProgressSink`.

## Anti-patterns that fail review

- `unwrap` / `expect` outside tests; `unsafe` anywhere (workspace forbids it).
- `#[allow(clippy::...)]` without a same-line reason.
- `as` casts between integer widths on untrusted values — use `try_from`.
- `to_string_lossy()` on a path that is later used to open or create a file.
- Ignoring compiler warnings or suppressing them crate-wide.
- Optimizing before measuring (see `rust-performance` skill).
